## Why This Is Asked

This question probes an advanced testing scenario where data consistency is eventual and involves a read-model updated by a streaming pipeline, requiring tests around read-your-writes, consistency window, multi-tenant isolation, and resilience to streaming outages.

## Key Concepts

- Eventual consistency between read-model and stream
- Read-your-writes and drift windows
- Per-tenant isolation under concurrency
- Streaming fault injection and recovery
- Contract testing across gateway and downstream services

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test sketch: verify eventual consistency
async function testReadYourWrites(tenantId, value){
  await writeMetric(tenantId, value);
  await untilTrue(async ()=> {
     const v = await readMetrics(tenantId);
     return v.includes(value);
  }, 30000);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure the real-world consistency window, and what metrics would you collect?
- How would you design roll-out and rollback strategies if drift exceeds SLA?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes multi-tenant privacy guarantees, real-time streaming accuracy, and admin-driven policy propagation—crucial for platforms handling sensitive audits.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation and access control
- Streaming backfill and latency budgets
- Admin-driven privacy filters and dynamic policy rollout
- Failure modes: Kafka outage, schema drift, topic compaction, replay semantics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test: verify tenant isolation in REST and Kafka stream
const tenant = 'tenantA';
const consumer = kafka.consumer({ groupId: 'audit-tenantA' });
await consumer.subscribe({ topic: 'audits', fromBeginning: true });
let received = [];
for await (const msg of consumer.consume()) {
  const ev = JSON.parse(msg.value.toString());
  if (ev.tenantId !== tenant) throw new Error('cross-tenant event detected');
  received.push(ev);
  if (received.length >= 5) break;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you quantify end-to-end latency from REST GET to stream emission?
- How would you test admin-flag propagation under high churn and network partitions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world idempotency handling, crucial for payment-like APIs.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency keys per tenant; per-tenant isolation; race-condition handling; retry semantics; error handling.

## Code Example
```javascript
// pseudocode: idempotency guard
if (exists(tenantId, idempotencyKey)) return cachedResponse;
beginTxn();
if (exists(tenantId, idempotencyKey)) return cachedResponse; // double-check
order = createOrder(payload);
cache(tenantId, idempotencyKey, order);
commitTxn();
return order;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test with network hiccups causing duplicate Idempotency-Key submissions?
- How would you monitor and alert on idempotency violations?
